  "editTargets": {
    "method": "<div class=\"section grow\" data-section=\"method\">\n        <h2>Method <button class=\"listen-btn\" data-section=\"method\">Listen</button></h2>\n        <ul>\n          <li><strong>Linearize &amp; embed</strong> the table with the question through the QA LLM's shared embedding layer.</li>\n          <li><strong>Unsupervised Relevance Scorer (URS)</strong> &mdash; a transformer encoder trained by variational inference with clustering, separation &amp; sparsification losses &mdash; scores every table token.</li>\n          <li><strong>Parsing-statement module</strong> (Flan-T5-XL, ~300 annotations) highlights relevant cells; the two scores are fused and multiply token embeddings before decoding.</li>\n        </ul>\n        <div class=\"p-eq\">$\\mathcal{L} = \\mathcal{L}_{CE} + \\lambda_{clu}\\mathcal{L}_{clu} + \\lambda_{sep}\\mathcal{L}_{sep} + \\lambda_{sparse}\\mathcal{L}_{sparse}$<span class=\"where\">fused weight $\\eta_p = \\lambda_{uns}\\,\\eta_p^{uns} + \\lambda_{cell}\\,\\eta_p^{cell}$ softly scales each token &mdash; nothing is deleted</span></div>\n        <figure><img src=\"assets/figures/figure2.png\" alt=\"half\"><figcaption>CABINET architecture: the unsupervised relevance score and the parsing-statement cell score are fused (steps 6&ndash;7) to weigh the table content passed to the QA LLM.</figcaption></figure>\n      </div>",
    "robustness": "<div class=\"section\" data-section=\"robustness\">\n        <h2>Robustness to Noise</h2>\n        <div class=\"p-key-stat\"><div class=\"num\">3.4&times;</div><div class=\"label\">smaller accuracy drop than OmniTab under column permutation</div></div>\n        <figure><img src=\"assets/figures/figure3.png\" alt=\"\"><figcaption>Performance drop (%) under Row-Add, Row/Col-Permute, Cell-Replace: CABINET (green) is far more robust to table noise than OmniTab (red).</figcaption></figure>\n      </div>",
    "key-result": "<div class=\"section grow\" data-section=\"key-result\">\n        <h2>Key Results <button class=\"listen-btn\" data-section=\"key-result\">Listen</button></h2>\n        <table class=\"p-table\">\n          <tr><th>Method (WikiTQ)</th><th>Acc.</th><th>&Delta;</th></tr>\n          <tr><td>OmniTab</td><td>62.7</td><td>&minus;6.4</td></tr>\n          <tr><td>Flan-T5-XL (fine-tuned)</td><td>64.4</td><td>&minus;4.7</td></tr>\n          <tr><td>DATER (GPT-3, in-context)</td><td>65.9</td><td>&minus;3.2</td></tr>\n          <tr class=\"best\"><td>CABINET (Ours)</td><td>69.1</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>\n        </table>\n        <div class=\"callout\">+6.4% over OmniTab &middot; new SoTA on WikiTQ, FeTaQA &amp; WikiSQL</div>\n        <p class=\"conclusion\">Soft relevance weighting beats hard-decomposition and larger GPT-3 / Codex in-context methods with only a 560M model.</p>\n      </div>",
    "headline-numbers": "<div class=\"section\" data-section=\"headline-numbers\">\n        <h2>Headline Numbers</h2>\n        <!-- Hero + supporting. 69.1% = signature number; WikiTQ accuracy \u00b7 new SoTA = what it measures;\n             +6.4% over OmniTab = italic punchline. \u22652 supporting .stat-mini tiles. Drop unused tiles if <4\n             numbers. Never invent \u2014 every value must trace to text.txt. -->\n        <div class=\"headline-hero\">\n          <div class=\"hero-main\"><div class=\"hero-val\">69.1%</div>\n          <div class=\"hero-label\">WikiTQ accuracy \u00b7 new SoTA</div>\n          <div class=\"hero-note\">+6.4% over OmniTab</div></div>\n          <div class=\"supporting\">\n            <div class=\"stat-mini\"><div class=\"val\">40.5</div><div class=\"lbl\">FeTaQA S-BLEU</div></div>\n            <div class=\"stat-mini\"><div class=\"val\">89.5%</div><div class=\"lbl\">WikiSQL acc.</div></div>\n            <div class=\"stat-mini\"><div class=\"val\">560M</div><div class=\"lbl\">parameters</div></div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </div>",
    "ablation-study": "<div class=\"section\" data-section=\"ablation-study\">\n        <h2>Ablation Study <button class=\"listen-btn\" data-section=\"ablation-study\">Listen</button></h2>\n        <ul>\n          <li><strong>URS losses work only together:</strong> clustering + separation + sparsification lift WikiTQ 60.8 &rarr; 65.6; any subset barely helps.</li>\n          <li><strong>Fuse both signals:</strong> &lambda;<sub>uns</sub>=0.7, &lambda;<sub>cell</sub>=0.3 gives 69.1; cell-based relevance alone collapses to 37.6.</li>\n        </ul>\n        <div class=\"p-callout-soft\">The unsupervised scorer is the primary driver; the parsing-statement module is a complementary aid.</div>\n      </div>",
    "takeaway": "<div class=\"section\" data-section=\"takeaway\">\n        <h2>Takeaway <button class=\"listen-btn\" data-section=\"takeaway\">Listen</button></h2>\n        <p>Softly weighting table content by learned relevance &mdash; instead of hard-decomposing the table &mdash; lets an LLM focus on the cells that matter, keeping full access to the data while suppressing noise.</p>\n        <div class=\"p-callout-primary\">Don't cut the table down &mdash; weight it. Soft relevance sets new state of the art on three table-QA benchmarks with a compact 560M model.</div>\n      </div>",
    "motivation": "<div class=\"section grow\" data-section=\"motivation\">\n        <h2>Motivation <button class=\"listen-btn\" data-section=\"motivation\">Listen</button></h2>\n        <p>A natural fix is to shrink the table first, but <strong>hard decomposition is unforgiving</strong>: pick the wrong sub-table and useful cells are lost for good.</p>\n        <div class=\"p-vs\">\n          <div class=\"side bad\"><h4>Hard decomposition (DATER)</h4><p>Extracts a sub-table; a wrong pick discards cells and locks in the error.</p></div>\n          <div class=\"sep\">vs.</div>\n          <div class=\"side good\"><h4>Soft weighting (CABINET)</h4><p>Weighs relevant cells higher, removes nothing, keeps the full table.</p></div>\n        </div>\n        <figure><img src=\"assets/figures/figure1.png\" alt=\"\"><figcaption>DATER hard-decomposes to a wrong sub-table and answers 38; CABINET soft-weights the relevant cells and answers 45 correctly.</figcaption></figure>\n      </div>"
  }
}